Reliquary Hustle

Ingredients: BEBOP + DISCO + ETHEREAL, DREAMY & AIRY + Music Box + 1990s ERA
Creative Catalyst: It grew out of a secluded Alpine monastery where cloistered monks transcribe their REM-sleep cycles directly into modular synth patches.

Reliquary Hustle sounds like a dizzying, miraculous fever: virtuosic, breakneck Charlie Parker horn lines and agile upright bass arpeggios crash headlong into a propulsive, glitter-drenched 1990s four-on-the-floor disco groove, all suspended inside a weightless sanctuary of music-box clockwork, tape-saturated air, and soaring monastic vocal sighs.

Genre DNA

- Tempo & Pulse: 122–128 BPM. The heartbeat is an unrelenting 1990s French-touch-leaning disco kick drum fused with skittering open hi-hats, juxtaposed against hyper-syncopated bebop walking basslines that refuse to sit squarely on the beat.
- Harmonic Palette: Labyrinthine bebop chord substitutions (flatted fifths, altered dominants, and sharp elevens) are softened and sustained into lush, dreamy modal washes; Dorian and Lydian drone pads drift underneath frantic ii-V-I saxophone cadenzas.
- Production Signatures:
  1. Tine-Modulation Sidechaining: Physical music boxes mic'd with contact pickups are heavily ducked beneath the disco kick, causing their fragile metallic pings to swell rhythmically in shimmering waves.
  2. Somno-CV Interfacing: Modular synth low-pass filter cutoffs are voltage-controlled by sleep-clinic EEG brainwave charts recorded from sleeping monks, introducing uncanny microtonal sweeps across chord changes.
  3. Lexicon Cathedral Gating: 1990s rackmount Lexicon reverbs set to astronomical 10-second decays, aggressively sliced and side-gated by 16th-note disco claps to create an illusion of infinite sacred space chopped into danceable fragments.
- Dynamics: Songs open as fragile music-box lullabies floating in tape hiss and liturgical room ambience, suddenly detonate into blistering, horn-fueled disco gallops, and periodically collapse into zero-gravity breakdowns where rapid-fire jazz improvisations hover over cathedral drone pads.

Scene & Origin Lore

In 1993, at the Abbey of Saint-Hilaire in the French Jura mountains, a splinter order of benedictine contemplatives partnered with burnt-out jazz instrumentalists and Parisian rave expatriates to conduct "the Great Transcription." Convinced that divine revelation hides within deep REM states, the monks hooked sleep-monitoring electrodes to early Doepfer Eurorack modular frames, translating sleeping brain spikes into voltage, melody, and rhythm. By 1996, bootlegged reel-to-reel tapes recorded in the abbey scriptorium escaped into the discotheques of Lyon and Zurich. The resulting subculture, known as Les Somnambules, dressed in flowing unbleached linen monk cowls lined with silver mirror-tile lamé and oversized 1990s wrap-around sport shades. Their parties—held strictly at dawn in deconsecrated basilicas—featured all-night communal sleep sessions followed by kinetic, sweat-drenched dancing where ravers tossed dried lavender into smoke machines and clapped along to blindingly complex saxophone solos.

Typical Instruments: 72-tooth mechanical cylinder music box (amplified), muted flugelhorn, Selmer Mark VI alto saxophone, fretless upright double bass, Roland TR-909 drum machine, Ensoniq ASR-10 sampler, Doepfer A-100 modular synthesizer, glass celesta, and Lexicon 480L digital reverb.

