- Story & Context: Confined to his solitary cell for three weeks of silence, Brother Hilaire used a smuggled battery-operated soldering iron to weld clockwork music-box cylinders into his modular rig, desperate to recreate the laughing voice of a dancer he had abandoned at a Paris warehouse party years before. The cell grew rank with the stench of cooling paraffin and stale chicory coffee as Hilaire plucked weeping, microtonal upright bass lines that tripped into an airy 125 BPM groove under the circular window's refracted indigo light. In the track’s hushed, weightless breakdown, his cracked baritone breaks monastic silence to admit his secret: "I let the glass crack so your music could come back in."
